            ('101', 'Election of members of Parliament', 'A general election of members of Parliament shall be held on the second Tuesday in August in every fifth year.'),
            ('102', 'Term of Parliament', 'The term of each House of Parliament is five years, from the date of the first sitting after a general election.'),
            ('103', 'Vacation of office of member of Parliament', 'The office of a member of Parliament becomes vacant if the member dies, resigns, is recalled by the electorate, is disqualified, or is absent from eight consecutive sittings without permission.'),
            ('104', 'Right of recall', 'The electorate under Articles 97 and 98 have the right to recall the member of Parliament representing their constituency before the end of the term.'),
            ('105', 'Determination of questions of membership', 'The High Court shall hear and determine any question whether a person has been validly elected as a member of Parliament, or whether the seat of a member has become vacant.'),
            ('106', 'Speakers and Deputy Speakers of Parliament', 'There shall be a Speaker for each House of Parliament, who shall be elected by that House from among persons who are qualified to be elected as members of Parliament but are not such members.'),
            ('107', 'Presiding in Parliament', 'At any sitting of a House of Parliament, the Speaker, the Deputy Speaker, or another member elected by the House shall preside.'),
            ('108', 'Party leaders', 'There shall be a leader of the majority party and a leader of the minority party in each House of Parliament.'),
            ('109', 'Exercise of legislative powers', 'Parliament shall exercise its legislative power through Bills passed by Parliament and assented to by the President. A Bill may be introduced in either House, but a Bill not concerning county government may be introduced only in the National Assembly.'),
            ('110', 'Bills concerning county government', 'A Bill concerning county government is one that relates to the election of members of a county assembly or county executive, or a matter affecting the functions and financing of county governments.'),
            ('111', 'Special Bills concerning county governments', 'A special Bill concerning county governments shall be introduced in the National Assembly and then referred to the Senate.'),
            ('112', 'Ordinary Bills concerning county governments', 'An ordinary Bill concerning county government may originate in either House, and after being passed by that House shall be considered by the other House.'),
            ('113', 'Mediation committees', 'If one House passes a Bill and the other House rejects it or passes it with amendments to which the originating House objects, the Bill shall be referred to a mediation committee.'),
            ('114', 'Money Bills', 'A Money Bill may be introduced only in the National Assembly. A Money Bill is one that deals with taxation, borrowing, appropriation, or other public money matters.'),
            ('115', 'Presidential assent and referral', 'Within fourteen days of receipt of a Bill, the President shall either assent to the Bill or refer it back to Parliament for reconsideration. If the President refers a Bill back, Parliament may amend it or pass it a second time with a two-thirds majority, after which the President shall assent.'),
            ('116', 'Coming into force of laws', 'A law comes into force on the fourteenth day after its publication in the Kenya Gazette, unless the law provides for a different date.'),
            ('117', 'Powers, privileges and immunities', 'There shall be freedom of speech and debate in Parliament. No civil or criminal proceedings may be instituted against a member for words spoken in Parliament.'),
            ('118', 'Public access and participation', 'Parliament shall conduct its business in an open manner, and its sittings shall be open to the public. Parliament shall facilitate public participation in the legislative process.'),
            ('119', 'Right to petition Parliament', 'Every person has a right to petition Parliament to consider any matter within its authority, including enacting, amending or repealing any legislation.'),
            ('120', 'Official languages of Parliament', 'The official languages of Parliament are Kiswahili and English. The business of Parliament may be conducted in either language.'),
            ('121', 'Quorum', 'The quorum of each House is at least fifty members of the National Assembly and fifteen members of the Senate.'),
            ('122', 'Voting in Parliament', 'Except as otherwise provided, any question proposed for decision in either House shall be determined by a majority of the members present and voting. The Speaker shall have a casting vote.'),
            ('123', 'Decisions of Senate', 'On any matter other than a Bill, the Senate may resolve that a decision be taken by a vote of the delegations, where each county delegation has one vote.'),
            ('124', 'Committees and Standing Orders', 'Each House of Parliament may establish committees and shall make Standing Orders for the orderly conduct of its proceedings.'),
            ('125', 'Power to call for evidence', 'Parliament has the power to call any person to appear before it for the purpose of giving evidence or providing information.'),
            ('126', 'Public officers to attend Parliament', 'Cabinet Secretaries and other public officers may attend and participate in the proceedings of Parliament but may not vote.'),
            ('127', 'Parliamentary Service Commission', 'There is established the Parliamentary Service Commission, which shall provide services and facilities to ensure the efficient and effective functioning of Parliament.'),
            ('128', 'Clerks and staff of Parliament', 'There shall be a Clerk for each House of Parliament, appointed by the Parliamentary Service Commission. The offices of the Clerks are offices in the Parliamentary Service.'),
